    "id": "topic-1789931380251",
    "category": "Python Basics",
    "title": "Casting",
    "content": "<p class=\"text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300\">Casting is used when you want to explicitly specify or convert the data type of a variable using constructor functions.</p>\n\n<h2 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-6 mb-3\">Explicit Casting Functions</h2>\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3 group\">\n    <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n    <pre><code># Convert to Integer: int()\nx = int(2.8)     # Converts float to int -> 2\ny = int(\"10\")    # Converts numeric string to int -> 10\n\n# Convert to Float: float()\na = float(1)     # Converts int to float -> 1.0\nb = float(\"4.2\") # Converts string to float -> 4.2\n\n# Convert to String: str()\ns1 = str(100)    # Converts int to string -> \"100\"\ns2 = str(3.14)   # Converts float to string -> \"3.14\"</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-6 mb-3\">Practical Use Case: User Input Conversion</h2>\n<p class=\"mb-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400\">The <code>input()</code> function returns a string by default, so numbers must be cast before doing math:</p>\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3 group\">\n    <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n    <pre><code># Prompting user for numeric input\nage_input = input(\"Enter your age: \") # Returns string e.g., \"20\"\n\n# Cast to integer for math operations\nage = int(age_input)\nnext_year_age = age + 1\n\nprint(f\"Next year you will be {next_year_age}.\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n"
  },
