    "title": "Numbers",
    "content": "<p class=\"text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300\">\n    Python has three main numeric types: <code>int</code>, <code>float</code>, and <code>complex</code>. \n    Numeric variables are created automatically when you assign a number to them.\n</p>\n\n<h2 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-6 mb-3\">The Three Numeric Types</h2>\n\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300\">\n    <li><code>int</code> — Integer numbers</li>\n    <li><code>float</code> — Floating-point numbers</li>\n    <li><code>complex</code> — Complex numbers</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3 group\">\n    <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\">\n        <i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy\n    </button>\n    <pre><code>x = 1       # int\ny = 2.8     # float\nz = 1j      # complex\n\nprint(x)\nprint(y)\nprint(z)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<h2 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-6 mb-3\">Checking the Number Type</h2>\n\n<p class=\"mb-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300\">\n    You can use the <code>type()</code> function to check the data type of a number.\n</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3 group\">\n    <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\">\n        <i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy\n    </button>\n    <pre><code>x = 1\ny = 2.8\nz = 1j\n\nprint(type(x))\nprint(type(y))\nprint(type(z))</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p class=\"mb-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300\">\n    Python will identify these as <code>int</code>, <code>float</code>, and <code>complex</code>.\n</p>\n\n<h2 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-6 mb-3\">Integers — int</h2>\n\n<p class=\"mb-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300\">\n    An <code>int</code>, or integer, is a whole number. It can be positive, negative, or zero, and it does not contain decimal places.\n</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3 group\">\n    <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\">\n        <i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy\n    </button>\n    <pre><code>x = 1\ny = 35656222554887711\nz = -3255522\n\nprint(type(x))\nprint(type(y))\nprint(type(z))</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n    <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\">\n        <i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Important:\n    </strong>\n    Python integers can be very large because Python integers have unlimited length, limited by available memory.\n</div>\n\n<h2 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-6 mb-3\">Floating-Point Numbers — float</h2>\n\n<p class=\"mb-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300\">\n    A <code>float</code>, or floating-point number, is a number that contains a decimal point. Floats can also be positive or negative.\n</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3 group\">\n    <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\">\n        <i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy\n    </button>\n    <pre><code>x = 1.10\ny = 1.0\nz = -35.59\n\nprint(type(x))\nprint(type(y))\nprint(type(z))</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-4 mb-2\">Scientific Notation</h3>\n\n<p class=\"mb-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300\">\n    Floats can also be written using scientific notation. The lette\n"
