    "id": "topic-1790010845467",
    "category": "Python Basics",
    "title": "For loops",
    "content": "<h1>Python For Loops</h1>\n\n<p>A <code>for</code> loop is used to iterate over a sequence such as a list, tuple, set, dictionary, or string.</p>\n\n<p>The <code>for</code> loop runs once for each item in the sequence.</p>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  Use a <code>for</code> loop when you want to repeat code for every item in a sequence.\n</div>\n\n<h2>The for Loop</h2>\n\n<p>The basic syntax of a <code>for</code> loop is:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>for variable in sequence:\n    # code to execute</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>Example:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>fruits = [\"apple\", \"banana\", \"orange\"]\n\nfor fruit in fruits:\nprint(fruit)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>apple\nbanana\norange</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>Python takes each item from <code>fruits</code>, stores it temporarily in <code>fruit</code>, and runs the code inside the loop.</p>\n\n<h2>Looping Through a String</h2>\n\n<p>A string is a sequence of characters, so you can use a <code>for</code> loop to go through each character.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>for letter in \"Python\":\n    print(letter)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>P\ny\nt\nh\no\nn</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  A <code>for</code> loop can iterate over each character in a string just like it iterates over each item in a list.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Using range()</h2>\n\n<p>The <code>range()</code> function creates a sequence of numbers that is often used with <code>for</code> loops.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>for i in range(5):\n    print(i)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>0\n1\n2\n3\n4</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p><code>range(5)</code> starts at <code>0</code> and stops before <code>5</code>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  The stop value in <code>range()</code> is not included. <code>range(5)</code> gives you <code>0</code> through <code>4</code>.\n</div>\n\n<h2>range() with a Start Value</h2>\n\n<p>You can give <code>range()</code> a starting value.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>for i in range(2, 6):\n    print(i)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>2\n3\n4\n5</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p><code>range(2, 6)</code> starts at <code>2</code> and stops before <code>6</code>.</p>\n\n<h2>range() with a Step</h2>\n\n<p>You can use a third argument to specify the step between numbers.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>for i in range(0, 10, 2):\n    print(i)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>0\n2\n4\n6\n8</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>The third value, <code>2</code>, tells Python to increase the number by 2 each time.</p>\n\n<h2>Counting Backwards</h2>\n\n<p>You can use a negative step to count backwards.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>for i in range(5, 0, -1):\n    print(i)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>5\n4\n3\n2\n1</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  A negative step makes <code>range()</code> count backwards.\n</div>\n\n<h2>The break Statement</h2>\n\n<p>The <code>break</code> statement stops the <code>for</code> loop immediately.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>for i in range(10):\n    if i == 5:\n        break\n\n```\nprint(i)</code></pre>\n```\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>0\n1\n2\n3\n4</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>When <code>i</code> becomes <code>5</code>, the <code>break</code> statement stops the loop.</p>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  Use <code>break</code> when you want to stop the loop before it reaches the end of the sequence.\n</div>\n\n<h2>The continue Statement</h2>\n\n<p>The <code>continue</code> statement skips the current iteration and moves to the next one.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>for i in range(6):\n    if i == 3:\n        continue\n\n```\nprint(i)</code></pre>\n```\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>0\n1\n2\n4\n5</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>When <code>i</code> is <code>3</code>, <code>continue</code> skips the <code>print()</code> statement and moves to the next iteration.</p>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  <code>break</code> stops the loop, while <code>continue</code> only skips the current iteration.\n</div>\n\n<h2>The else Statement</h2>\n\n<p>A <code>for</code> loop can have an <code>else</code> statement.</p>\n\n<p>The <code>else</code> block runs when the loop finishes normally.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>for i in range(5):\n    print(i)\nelse:\n    print(\"Loop finished\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>0\n1\n2\n3\n4\nLoop finished</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  The <code>else</code> block does not run when the loop is stopped with <code>break</code>.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Nested for Loops</h2>\n\n<p>You can place one <code>for</code> loop inside another <code>for</code> loop.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>for i in range(3):\n    for j in range(2):\n        print(i, j)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>The inner loop completes all of its iterations for each iteration of the outer loop.</p>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>0 0\n0 1\n1 0\n1 1\n2 0\n2 1</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Looping Through a List</h2>\n\n<p>You can use a <code>for</code> loop to access each item in a list.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>names = [\"John\", \"Michael\", \"Sarah\"]\n\nfor name in names:\nprint(name)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<h2>Looping Through a Tuple</h2>\n\n<p>You can also loop through a tuple.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>numbers = (10, 20, 30)\n\nfor number in numbers:\nprint(number)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<h2>Looping Through a Set</h2>\n\n<p>A <code>for</code> loop can iterate through a set.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>colours = {\"red\", \"blue\", \"green\"}\n\nfor colour in colours:\nprint(colour)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  Sets are unordered, so you should not rely on a particular iteration order.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Looping Through a Dictionary</h2>\n\n<p>By default, a <code>for</code> loop through a dictionary iterates over its keys.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>person = {\n    \"name\": \"Michael\",\n    \"age\": 16\n}\n\nfor key in person:\nprint(key)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>You can use <code>values()</code> to loop through the values.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>person = {\n    \"name\": \"Michael\",\n    \"age\": 16\n}\n\nfor value in person.values():\nprint(value)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>You can use <code>items()</code> to loop through both keys and values.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>person = {\n    \"name\": \"Michael\",\n    \"age\": 16\n}\n\nfor key, value in person.items():\nprint(key, value)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<h2>Using enumerate()</h2>\n\n<p>The <code>enumerate()</code> function lets you access both the index and the value while looping.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>fruits = [\"apple\", \"banana\", \"orange\"]\n\nfor index, fruit in enumerate(fruits):\nprint(index, fruit)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>0 apple\n1 banana\n2 orange</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  <code>enumerate()</code> is useful when you need both the position and the value of each item.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Using zip()</h2>\n\n<p>The <code>zip()</code> function allows you to loop through multiple sequences at the same time.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>names = [\"John\", \"Sarah\", \"Michael\"]\nages = [15, 17, 16]\n\nfor name, age in zip(names, ages):\nprint(name, age)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>John 15\nSarah 17\nMichael 16</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>List Comprehension</h2>\n\n<p>Python allows you to create a new list using a <code>for</code> loop in a single line. This is called a list comprehension.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n\nsquares = [number ** 2 for number in numbers]\n\nprint(squares)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>[1, 4, 9, 16, 25]</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  List comprehensions are useful for simple transformations, but a normal <code>for</code> loop can be easier to read for more complicated logic.\n</div>\n\n<h2>for Loop with an if Statement</h2>\n\n<p>You can use an <code>if</code> statement inside a <code>for</code> loop.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\n\nfor number in numbers:\nif number % 2 == 0:\nprint(number)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>2\n4\n6</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>The <code>if</code> statement checks each number and only prints the even numbers.</p>\n\n<h2>for Loop with User Input</h2>\n\n<p>You can use <code>for</code> loops together with <code>input()</code>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>number = int(input(\"Enter a number: \"))\n\nfor i in range(1, 11):\nprint(number * i)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>This example prints the multiplication table for the number entered by the user.</p>\n\n<h2>Using pass</h2>\n\n<p>A <code>for</code> loop cannot have an empty block. You can use <code>pass</code> when you need a placeholder.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>for i in range(5):\n    pass</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  <code>pass</code> does nothing. It is useful as a placeholder while you are building your program.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Example</h2>\n\n<p>Here is a simple program that checks a list of numbers:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>numbers = [10, 15, 20, 25, 30]\n\nfor number in numbers:\nif number >= 20:\nprint(number, \"is 20 or greater\")\nelse:\nprint(number, \"is less than 20\")</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>The loop checks every number in the list and uses the <code>if...else</code> statement to decide what to print.</p>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  A <code>for</code> loop is often combined with <code>if</code> statements to filter or process specific items.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Summary</h2>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  A <code>for</code> loop repeats code for each item in a sequence.<br>\n  Use <code>range()</code> when you need a sequence of numbers.<br>\n  Use <code>break</code> to stop the loop.<br>\n  Use <code>continue</code> to skip the current iteration.<br>\n  A <code>for</code> loop can have an <code>else</code> block.<br>\n  <code>enumerate()</code> gives you both the index and the value.<br>\n  <code>zip()</code> lets you iterate through multiple sequences together.<br>\n  A <code>for</code> loop can be nested inside another <code>for</code> loop.\n</div>\n"
