    "content": "<h1>Python Functions</h1>\n\n<p>A function is a block of code that only runs when it is called.</p>\n\n<p>You can pass data, known as parameters, into a function. A function can also return data.</p>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  Functions help you organise your code and avoid repeating the same code multiple times.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Creating a Function</h2>\n\n<p>In Python, you create a function using the <code>def</code> keyword.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def my_function():\n    print(\"Hello from my function\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>The function is now defined, but it will not run until you call it.</p>\n\n<h2>Calling a Function</h2>\n\n<p>To call a function, write the function name followed by parentheses.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def my_function():\n    print(\"Hello from my function\")\n\nmy_function()</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>Hello from my function</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  Defining a function does not execute it. You must call the function to run its code.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Function Parameters</h2>\n\n<p>You can pass information into a function using parameters.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def greet(name):\n    print(\"Hello\", name)\n\ngreet(\"Michael\")</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>Hello Michael</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>In this example, <code>name</code> is the parameter and <code>\"Michael\"</code> is the argument passed to the function.</p>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  A parameter is the variable in the function definition. An argument is the value you pass when calling the function.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Multiple Parameters</h2>\n\n<p>A function can have more than one parameter.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def greet(first_name, last_name):\n    print(\"Hello\", first_name, last_name)\n\ngreet(\"Michael\", \"Okoroafor\")</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<h2>Default Parameter Values</h2>\n\n<p>You can give a parameter a default value.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def greet(name=\"Guest\"):\n    print(\"Hello\", name)\n\ngreet()\ngreet(\"Michael\")</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>Hello Guest\nHello Michael</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  A default value is used when no argument is provided for that parameter.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Keyword Arguments</h2>\n\n<p>You can send arguments using the parameter names.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def person(name, age):\n    print(name, age)\n\nperson(age=16, name=\"Michael\")</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Because the parameter names are specified, the order of the arguments does not matter.</p>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  Keyword arguments can make function calls easier to understand, especially when a function has many parameters.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Passing a List to a Function</h2>\n\n<p>You can pass a list as an argument to a function.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def print_fruits(fruits):\n    for fruit in fruits:\n        print(fruit)\n\nfruits = [\"apple\", \"banana\", \"orange\"]\n\nprint_fruits(fruits)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<h2>Returning a Value</h2>\n\n<p>The <code>return</code> statement sends a value back from a function.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nresult = add(5, 3)\n\nprint(result)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>8</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  <code>return</code> ends the function and sends a value back to the code that called it.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Returning Multiple Values</h2>\n\n<p>A function can return multiple values. Python packages them into a tuple.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def calculate(a, b):\n    return a + b, a - b\n\naddition, subtraction = calculate(10, 5)\n\nprint(addition)\nprint(subtraction)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<h2>Arbitrary Arguments</h2>\n\n<p>If you do not know how many arguments will be passed, use <code>*args</code>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def names(*names):\n    for name in names:\n        print(name)\n\nnames(\"John\", \"Sarah\", \"Michael\")</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>The arguments are collected into a tuple.</p>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  The name <code>args</code> is a convention. The important part is the <code>*</code>, which collects positional arguments into a tuple.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Arbitrary Keyword Arguments</h2>\n\n<p>If you do not know how many keyword arguments will be passed, use <code>**kwargs</code>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def person(**details):\n    print(details)\n\nperson(name=\"Michael\", age=16)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>The keyword arguments are collected into a dictionary.</p>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  The name <code>kwargs</code> is a convention. The important part is the <code>**</code>, which collects keyword arguments into a dictionary.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Combining Parameters</h2>\n\n<p>You can combine normal parameters, <code>*args</code>, and <code>**kwargs</code> in one function.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def example(name, *args, **kwargs):\n    print(\"Name:\", name)\n    print(\"Args:\", args)\n    print(\"Kwargs:\", kwargs)\n\nexample(\"Michael\", 10, 20, age=16, country=\"UK\")</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<h2>Positional-Only Arguments</h2>\n\n<p>You can specify that certain arguments must be passed by position using <code>/</code>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def person(name, age, /):\n    print(name, age)\n\nperson(\"Michael\", 16)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Using a keyword argument for either parameter would cause an error.</p>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  The <code>/</code> marker means parameters before it are positional-only.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Keyword-Only Arguments</h2>\n\n<p>You can specify that certain arguments must be passed using their parameter names by using <code>*</code>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def person(name, *, age):\n    print(name, age)\n\nperson(\"Michael\", age=16)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  Parameters after <code>*</code> must be passed as keyword arguments.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Scope in Functions</h2>\n\n<p>A variable created inside a function normally belongs to that function and is called a local variable.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def my_function():\n    message = \"Hello\"\n    print(message)\n\nmy_function()</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>The variable <code>message</code> cannot normally be accessed outside the function.</p>\n\n<h2>Global Variables</h2>\n\n<p>A variable created outside a function is a global variable.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>message = \"Hello\"\n\ndef my_function():\nprint(message)\n\nmy_function()</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<h2>The global Keyword</h2>\n\n<p>The <code>global</code> keyword can be used when you need to modify a global variable inside a function.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>count = 0\n\ndef increase():\nglobal count\ncount += 1\n\nincrease()\n\nprint(count)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  Avoid using <code>global</code> unless you actually need to modify global state. Returning values is often a cleaner approach.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Passing Different Data Types</h2>\n\n<p>Functions can accept many different Python data types.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def show_value(value):\n    print(value)\n\nshow_value(10)\nshow_value(\"Hello\")\nshow_value([1, 2, 3])\nshow_value(True)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<h2>Docstrings</h2>\n\n<p>A docstring is a string placed at the beginning of a function to describe what the function does.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def greet(name):\n    \"\"\"Prints a greeting.\"\"\"\n    print(\"Hello\", name)\n\ngreet(\"Michael\")</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>You can access a function's docstring using <code>__doc__</code>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>print(greet.__doc__)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Type Hints</h2>\n\n<p>Python allows you to add type hints to function parameters and return values.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def add(a: int, b: int) -&gt; int:\n    return a + b\n\nresult = add(5, 3)\n\nprint(result)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  Type hints document the expected types, but Python does not automatically enforce them at runtime.\n</div>\n\n<h2>The pass Statement</h2>\n\n<p>A function cannot have an empty body. You can use <code>pass</code> as a placeholder.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def my_function():\n    pass</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Recursion</h2>\n\n<p>A function can call itself. This is known as recursion.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def countdown(number):\n    if number &gt; 0:\n        print(number)\n        countdown(number - 1)\n\ncountdown(5)</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>The function keeps calling itself with a smaller value until the condition becomes <code>False</code>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  Recursive functions need a stopping condition, otherwise they can continue calling themselves until Python raises a <code>RecursionError</code>.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Lambda Functions</h2>\n\n<p>A lambda function is a small anonymous function that can take any number of arguments but can only contain one expression.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>add = lambda a, b: a + b\n\nprint(add(5, 3))</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Output:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <pre><code>8</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  Use a normal <code>def</code> function when the function needs multiple statements or more complex logic.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Nested Functions</h2>\n\n<p>A function can be defined inside another function.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def outer():\n    def inner():\n        print(\"Hello from inner function\")\n\n```\ninner()\n```\n\nouter()</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<h2>Functions Calling Other Functions</h2>\n\n<p>A function can call another function.</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\ndef show_result():\nresult = add(10, 5)\nprint(result)\n\nshow_result()</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<h2>Example</h2>\n\n<p>Here is a simple calculator using functions:</p>\n\n<div class=\"bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl relative my-3\">\n  <button onclick=\"copyCode(this)\" class=\"absolute top-2.5 right-2.5 px-2 py-1 text-xs bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-300 rounded border border-slate-700\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\ndef subtract(a, b):\nreturn a - b\n\ndef multiply(a, b):\nreturn a * b\n\ndef divide(a, b):\nreturn a / b\n\nprint(add(10, 5))\nprint(subtract(10, 5))\nprint(multiply(10, 5))\nprint(divide(10, 5))</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<h2>Summary</h2>\n\n<div class=\"my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm\">\n  <strong class=\"font-bold flex items-center gap-1.5 mb-1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip:</strong>\n  Use <code>def</code> to create a function.<br>\n  Call a function using its name followed by <code>()</code>.<br>\n  Parameters allow functions to receive data.<br>\n  <code>return</code> sends a value back from a function.<br>\n  Default parameters provide fallback values.<br>\n  Keyword arguments use parameter names when calling a function.<br>\n  <code>*args</code> collects extra positional arguments into a tuple.<br>\n  <code>**kwargs</code> collects extra keyword arguments into a dictionary.<br>\n  <code>pass</code> can be used as a placeholder for an empty function.<br>\n  A function can call itself using recursion.\n</div>\n"
  },
